
  Start every job of a profile, in its order, in each worktree named, or in the one you stand in.
  It starts --profile, else run.toml's default profile, else its only one; a run.toml with no
  profile starts every job. Several worktrees start at once, each on its own: one that fails
  leaves the others running, and the run exits 1.

  A task runs to its end before the next job starts, and its failure stops that worktree's start;
  a service is launched and left running. A shared service runs once, in the main checkout, and
  each worktree holds it. Once the jobs are up, each port they declare is checked, and a service
  that ended since fails the start (--no-probe and run.toml's port_probe_timeout skip the check).

  When other worktrees run jobs, it asks whether to stop them first, unless --exclusive,
  --parallel or run.toml's concurrency answers; --yes keeps them. A job whose touches reach data
  the worktree does not own is refused unless --force.

  Usage:
    wtm-next run up [worktree...] [flags]

  Examples:
    # The default profile, in the worktree you stand in
    wtm run up

    # Two worktrees, another profile
    wtm run up feat/login fix/typo --profile backend

    # For a script or an agent, with the v2 JSON envelope
    wtm run up feat/login --yes --output json

  Flags:
        --ask                         Ask again the questions this repository remembers an answer to, to change or forget it
    -d, --detach                      Return once the jobs are started (the command line shows no live view yet)
    -n, --dry-run                     Show the plan and its fingerprint, apply nothing
        --exclusive                   Stop the jobs the other worktrees run before starting (one worktree only)
        --expect-fingerprint string   Apply only if the facts still have this fingerprint, the one --dry-run printed
        --force                       Start a job whose touches reach data the worktree does not own (its source's, or a shared service's with no namespace)
    -h, --help                        help for up
        --no-probe                    Skip the check that each declared port answers
        --output string               Output format: text or json (default "text")
        --parallel                    Keep the other worktrees' jobs running, whatever run.toml's concurrency says
        --profile string              The profile of run.toml to start (default: the one marked default, or the only one)
    -y, --yes                         Run unattended: no question, no confirmation (the worktree you stand in and run.toml's default profile unless given; the other worktrees' jobs keep running unless --exclusive)

  Global Flags:
    -q, --quiet   Silence human output; errors and the exit code are unaffected, and --output json still emits its document

